    }
    generator_model: MODEL_GOOGLE_GEMINI_INTERNAL_BYOM
    execution_id: "5ae020b5-bbf0-4b61-8e1b-9bd779d10716"
    source_trajectory_step_info {
      trajectory_id: "5ae020b5-bbf0-4b61-8e1b-9bd779d10716"
      step_index: 11
      metadata_index: 4
      cascade_id: "5ae020b5-bbf0-4b61-8e1b-9bd779d10716"
    }
    step_generation_version: 1
    model_info {
      model_name: "gemini-3.6-flash"
      thinking_budget: 4000
    }
    started_at {
      seconds: 1785686385
      nanos: 760937139
    }
  }
  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Defining Core Coordinates**\n\nI\'m currently focused on establishing the fundamental data structures, specifically the coordinate system for the PCB trace router. I\'ve defined coordinates as (layer, row, col) to accommodate single or dual-layer boards, using vias for layer transitions. This foundational definition will inform the rest of the architecture.\n\n\n**Structuring Board Data**\n\nI\'ve formalized the PCB board data structure. I\'m storing board dimensions, obstacles, and net lists. Nets include sources, targets, and priority levels. The data structure is now defined with key attributes for tracking layout features.\n\n\n**Implementing Conflict Resolution**\n\nI\'m now implementing the rip-up and reroute logic for conflict resolution. Shortest-first routing is the initial strategy. When a net gets blocked, it will analyze conflicting nets using a Union-Find data structure. I\'m focusing on identifying blockers, ripping them up, and then rerouting the initial blocked net. The number of rounds will be bounded to prevent infinite loops. Key metrics I\'m tracking include total wirelength, via usage, explored cells, and execution time.\n\n\n**Designing UI Screens**\n\nI\'m now designing the interactive UI screens. I\'ve broken it into two main parts: a Board Editor and a Routing Console with a live animator. The Board Editor has controls for board dimensions, obstacle density, and a grid canvas for editing the PCB layout. I\'ve included presets for testing and a random board generator. The Routing Console offers controls for running different routing algorithms and a live animator for visualizing the routing process. It also contains playback controls such as play, pause, and speed adjustment. The results displayed will include completion status, rip-up rounds, and conflict cluster data.\n\n\n**Developing Visualization Components**\n\nI\'m now expanding the UI with advanced visualization features. I\'m focusing on highlighting visited cells, displaying directional arrows or distance values, and providing a detailed search log. This will dramatically improve debugging.\nI\'m also building a \"Comparison Dashboard\" with side-by-side metric tables and interactive charts, visualizing Lee\'s, A*, and Rip-Up algorithms. A crucial aspect is enabling exports for the routing results in JSON and KiCad formats. Lastly, I\'m integrating a congestion heatmap.\n\n\n**Integrating Routing Modules**\n\nI\'ve integrated the A* algorithm, using a min-heap, Manhattan distance, and bend penalties. The Rip-Up-and-Reroute controller, employing Union-Find for conflict resolution, is now online. I\'ve built a shared facade to run Lee, A*, and Rip-Up for comparison, which will create reports. Preset boards for testing are also included.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 10
      nanos: 24266647
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
